    <link type="seealso" xref="elements"/>
    <desc>Linear, postal and two-dimensional barcodes, drawn bar for bar as
    the printer prints them.</desc>
  </info>

  <title>Barcodes</title>

  <p><gui>+ Barcode</gui> adds a Code 128 barcode. Choose another kind with
  <gui>Symbology</gui>, and type what it holds in <gui>Barcode
  Value</gui>. The canvas draws the symbol module for module, so its size on
  the canvas is its size on the label. In ZPL it is <code>^BY</code>, which
  sets the module width, then the barcode's own command, such as
  <code>^BC</code> for Code 128.</p>

  <section id="size">
    <title>How big it prints, and why it won't stretch</title>
    <p>A barcode is made of <em>modules</em>: the narrowest bar or space, or
    one square of a two-dimensional code. Every module is a whole number of
    dots, and everything else in the symbol is a whole number of modules. So
    a barcode can't be stretched to fill a box the way a picture can:</p>
    <list>
      <item><p>Its box is the bars, plus the printed value beneath or above
      them.</p></item>
      <item><p>Its width comes from encoding the value. More characters make
      it wider, and different characters can take different widths. Code
      128's <gui>Automatic</gui> mode packs runs of digits two to a symbol,
      which makes a number much shorter.</p></item>
      <item><p>To make it wider or narrower, change <gui>Module Width</gui>,
      or drag a side handle, which does the same. To make it taller, change
      <gui>Bar Height</gui> or drag the top or bottom.</p></item>
    </list>
    <p>A module of 2 dots is 0.25 mm on a 203 dpi printer, 0.17 mm at 300 dpi
    and 0.08 mm at 600 dpi. A scanner has a smallest module it can read, so
    check what yours, or your customer's, needs before shrinking a
    barcode.</p>
    <p>A linear barcode also needs a blank margin, the <em>quiet zone</em>,
    either side, where a scanner finds where the bars start and end. For
    Code 128 and Code 39 it is at least ten modules wide. The box does not
    include it, so keep other elements and the label's edge that far
    away.</p>
    <p>A two-dimensional code, such as a QR Code or Data Matrix, is a grid
    whose size the data decides: more data, or more error correction, makes
    a bigger grid. <gui>Magnification</gui> (<gui>Module Size</gui> for
    Data Matrix) sets how many dots each square is. Dragging a corner takes
    the largest size that fits the box you dragged.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="kinds">
    <title>The kinds offered</title>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title>Linear</title>
        <p>Code 128, Code 39, Code 93, Code 11, Codabar, Interleaved 2 of 5,
        Industrial 2 of 5, Standard 2 of 5, LOGMARS, MSI, Plessey, EAN-13,
        EAN-8, UPC-A, UPC-E, the UPC/EAN Extension, and GS1 DataBar.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>Postal</title>
        <p>POSTAL (Postnet, PLANET and the USPS Intelligent Mail barcode)
        and Planet Code.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>Two-dimensional</title>
        <p>QR Code, Data Matrix, PDF417, MicroPDF417, Aztec Code, MaxiCode
        and TLC39.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <note>
      <p>Of GS1 DataBar's twelve types, the canvas draws the UPC, EAN and
      GS1-128 ones. The DataBar types themselves (Omnidirectional to
      Expanded) are saved and sent to the printer, but the canvas shows only
      their box.</p>
    </note>
  </section>

  <section id="rows">
    <title>Settings</title>
    <p>Only the settings the chosen kind has are shown.</p>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Bar Height</gui></title>
        <p>The height of the bars, in dots. For PDF417 and MicroPDF417 it is
        the height of each row. QR Code, Data Matrix, Aztec Code and MaxiCode
        have none: their size comes from their data.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Module Width</gui></title>
        <p>The width of the narrowest bar, in dots, 1 to 20. Everything else
        in the symbol is a multiple of it. QR Code and Aztec Code call it
        <gui>Magnification</gui> and Data Matrix <gui>Module Size</gui>: the
        size of each square. MaxiCode has none.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Ratio</gui></title>
        <p>How much wider the wide bars are than the narrow ones, for the
        kinds that have both widths, such as Code 39 and Interleaved 2 of
        5.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Value Text</gui> and <gui>Text Height</gui></title>
        <p>Whether the value is printed in words <gui>Below the bars</gui>,
        <gui>Above the bars</gui>, or <gui>Not printed</gui>, and how
        tall.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>The check digit</title>
        <p>Its row is named for the kind. <gui>UCC Check Digit</gui> (Code
        128), <gui>Mod-43 Check Digit</gui> (Code 39) and <gui>Mod-10 Check
        Digit</gui> (Interleaved 2 of 5) add one. UPC-A, UPC-E, Code 93 and
        Plessey always carry one, so <gui>Print Check Digit</gui>,
        <gui>Print Check Characters</gui> and <gui>Print Check Digits</gui>
        only say whether the printed value shows it. MSI has <gui>Check
        Digits</gui>, which of its checksums to add, and <gui>Show Check
        Digits</gui>.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Mode</gui></title>
        <p>For Code 128. <gui>Automatic (uses subset C for digits)</gui>
        packs runs of digits two to a symbol, making the barcode
        shorter.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Orientation</gui></title>
        <p>Turns the barcode, as it turns <link xref="text">text</link>.
        Turned 90° or 270°, its box's width and height swap, and the side
        handles then set the bar height.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Variable</gui></title>
        <p>Tick <gui>Data comes from a numbered field (^FN)</gui> to have the
        barcode's value given each time the label is printed, as a numbered
        text field's is. <gui>Field Number</gui> and <gui>Field Name</gui>
        are as for text: see <link xref="templates"/>.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Reverse</gui> and <gui>ID</gui></title>
        <p>As for <link xref="text">text</link>.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <p>Dragging a handle changes the module width and the bar height; the
    barcode is never stretched to fill the box.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="fixed-length">
    <title>Values the barcode changes</title>
    <p>EAN-13 takes the last twelve digits of its value, padded on the left
    with zeros if there are fewer, and adds its own check digit. The UPC/EAN
    Extension fits its value to two digits or five in the same way. Neither
    refuses a value of the wrong length. Interleaved 2 of 5 needs an even
    number of digits, so a zero is put in front of an odd number. The canvas
    and the printed value show the digits as the barcode holds them.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="special">
    <title>Kinds with settings of their own</title>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title>MaxiCode</title>
        <p>Always the same size, about an inch across, at every resolution,
        so it has no module width, orientation or handles. <gui>Insert</gui> has buttons for the
        GS, RS and EOT characters its message uses, which no keyboard
        types.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>PDF417 and MicroPDF417</title>
        <p>Their <gui>Bar Height</gui> is the height of each row. A message
        too long for one symbol can be split over several:
        <gui>Positions (^FM)</gui> lists where each symbol goes, with
        <gui>Add</gui> and <gui>Remove</gui>. A barcode split this way has no
        handles; its symbols' sizes come from the rows.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>TLC39</title>
        <p>A Code 39 and a small MicroPDF417 together. The usual rows set the
        Code 39, and two more the MicroPDF417. Two symbols with different
        module widths can't share one drag, so it has no handles.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>

  <p>A barcode whose value cannot be encoded, such as data too long for a QR
  Code, keeps its place on the canvas but draws nothing, as the printer
  would print nothing.</p>

  <p>On a printer of another resolution, a barcode can't keep its size
  exactly: a module of 2 dots becomes 3, not 2.96. See
  <link xref="resolution#rescale"/>.</p>
</page>
